1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
A marketplace adding Pay with USDC
needs more than a wallet button.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:18,000
Settle coordinates planning, wallet handoff, Arc USDC funding,
escrow, programmable settlement, and reconciliation.

3
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:25,000
Here's the marketplace view.
The order total is exactly 0.05 USDC.

4
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:34,000
Pay with USDC moves to Settle's planning surface
without creating an onchain order.

5
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:44,500
The backend sends buyer, amount, deadlines, and a 90 / 10 split
to POST /api/v1/orders/plan.

6
00:00:44,500 --> 00:00:55,000
This is the demo's only live action. It is non-mutating:
no chain write, Circle mutation, or database mutation.

7
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:04,000
Settle returns an order ID, terms hash,
exact amount, and normalized settlement terms.

8
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:15,000
executionAvailable is false. This plan is not an onchain order.
Controlled activation is a separate server-side boundary.

9
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:25,500
Hosted checkout carries canonical context:
0.05 USDC on Arc Testnet, split 90 / 10.

10
00:01:25,500 --> 00:01:36,000
The QR contains only the checkout URL. Scanning opens Settle;
the wallet authorizes payment. The QR transfers nothing.

11
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:45,000
The monorepo separates web, domain code, server-side Circle
integration, contracts, and documentation.

12
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:56,000
This is actual Circle SDK code. Clients and validated
configuration live server-side in packages/circle.

13
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:07,000
Developer-Controlled Wallet calls use configured wallet identities.
Credentials never enter browser code. Contract Platform is here too.

14
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:14,000
Settle validates the funded order
and prepares releaseOrder.

15
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:21,000
Settle decides the action. Circle's controlled wallet submits it;
Circle does not decide settlement.

16
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:30,000
fundOrder accepts only the stored buyer before the deadline,
then transfers the exact Arc USDC amount.

17
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:39,000
A balance check rejects fee-on-transfer behavior.
The contract is the canonical escrow and lifecycle boundary.

18
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:48,000
releaseOrder requires a funded order. Stored basis-point terms
distribute the full amount deterministically.

19
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:57,000
The release closes active escrow. Refund and dispute paths
preserve explicit marketplace lifecycle semantics.

20
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:08,000
Buyer safety gates account, Arc chain, allowance,
canonical state, and readiness. Approve and fund stay wallet-controlled.

21
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:16,000
Testing exposed an idle-operation gating edge case,
now protected by focused regression coverage.

22
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:23,000
85 web and 167 Foundry tests pass.
252 total.

23
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:33,000
The immutable order is Completed.
The original 0.05 USDC is no longer active escrow.

24
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:43,000
Its programmed split produced 0.045 USDC for the merchant
and 0.005 USDC for the platform.

25
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:52,500
ArcScan shows the real release against verified
SettlementEscrow on Arc Testnet.

26
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:02,000
Transfer logs match both recipients and the full order amount.
Settle used its server-side Circle-controlled operator path.

27
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:12,000
The verified contract record confirms its address and Arc context.
This demonstration needs no new transaction or wallet interaction.

28
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:21,000
Docs cover quickstart, API, checkout, QR lifecycle,
settlement, Arc configuration, and boundaries.

29
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:30,000
Current integrations are USDC, Developer-Controlled Wallets,
and Circle Contract Platform. Other Circle products are planned.

30
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
Settle connects Payment Intent, Arc USDC, escrow,
programmable settlement, and reconciliation.

31
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
Circle stays at the controlled server boundary.
Payment infrastructure, not just an escrow interface.